# Revision Patterns

Generalizable patterns observed during editorial revision of blog posts.

## Link Quality: Illustration vs. Footnote

A link should *illustrate* its anchor text, not just prove the thing exists. If a reader clicks it, they should see something that enriches the sentence they just read.

- **Illustrative:** linking an error message to the issue that explains why it happens; linking a CSS property to the MDN page that explains the behavior being discussed.
- **Footnote:** linking "optional padding on edge-label containers" to a release changelog; linking "config restructure" to a single source file.

Inline code/snippets when **illustrating** a point. Link out to full files when providing a reference the reader might optionally explore. The difference between a book illustration and a bibliography entry.

## Provenance of Quoted Output

Don't include log snippets, error messages, or terminal output that wasn't actually observed. If a representative example comes from an external source (e.g., a GitHub issue), either attribute it or don't use it. Fabricating plausible output undermines trust even when the technical point is correct.

## Link Target Should Match Narrative Context

When *introducing* a tool or product to the reader, link to its product/landing page (e.g., rubygems.org, project homepage). When discussing code changes or implementation details deeper in the post, link to the source repository or specific files. Match the link target to what the reader needs at that point in the narrative.

## Shape Discipline on Tangents

Details should serve the post's chosen shape. An interesting anecdote that isn't part of the fable's cascade, the essay's argument, or the record's factual account should probably cut - unless it has a high value of its own. If a detail just happened to happen concurrently but wasn't caused by or required for anything in the narrative's chain, it's a tangent.

A prior-post thread that does not create a step for this reader is one incidental link, or a cut. "If you already did X, do nothing" is not a step. Repeating it does not make it load-bearing.

## Promise and Payoff

If the draft offers a tab, a recipe, or "I will get back to that," later cuts cannot leave the other half unpaid. Strike both, or deliver both.

## Housemate Knowledge

Assumed knowledge is for a stranger. A house nickname ("the Asus") needs a first intro. A lecture that only a housemate needed ("do not call this one the Pi") is a cut.

## Don't Write the Bug as the Recipe

A footgun in the imperative reads as steps. 

> Point a DNS name at the house WAN. Do not add that name as a WireGuard peer. Delete that row

is a procedure a stranger will follow, including the mistake. Instead, label the mistake in the first person (e.g. "I added it as a LuCI peer anyway"), then give the instruction they should actually run.

## Cuts Move Jobs

When you delete a lecture, check whether a later sentence was still using it. A cut that explained `wan` means house will make `Allow-WG` from `wan` look like an internet hole. The job has to land somewhere: a short gloss on the sentence that needs it, not a second copy of the lecture.

## Name the Concrete Difference

When the draft says one thing differs from another - newer, faster, exempt, unaffected, better - name the part, the version, the date, or the mechanism that makes the difference real. If you can't name what makes the difference, you can't claim the difference exists.

- WRONG: "The system Chrome rendered the diagrams differently than Puppeteer's did."
- RIGHT: "GitHub Actions' system Chrome measured text 11-16% narrower than Puppeteer's bundled Chromium, so edge labels landed in foreignObject containers too narrow for the text real browsers render."

## Sentence Rhythm

Read the draft aloud. When three sentences in a row land at nearly the same length, the prose has flattened toward the uniform mid-length cadence that long machine-written passages drift into. Break the run: cut one line to a fragment, or fold two into a single longer analytical sentence. Hand-written sections vary their rhythm without trying; agent-assisted drafts lose it over distance, so treat this as a revision check rather than a drafting concern.

When rythm changes, attention is drawn - you can use this to your advantage to highlight key points. If you overuse it, the impact is lost.

## Repeated Section Templates

Shape Discipline above asks whether a detail belongs; this asks whether the layout has gone mechanical. Three consecutive sections built from the same template - same opening move, same paragraph count, same closing beat - read as generated even when every fact in them is true. Vary how sections open and how far they run.

## Keep the Connectors in Your Speaking Register

This is the one spot where a flat list beats a principle, because these phrases carry no meaning your plain words don't. On a revision pass, swap them on sight for the word you'd use talking to a colleague.

- Transitions: prefer *also / but / however / still / and* over "Furthermore", "Moreover", "That being said", "At its core", "In essence", "It's worth noting that".
- Diction: prefer *before / after / about / because* over "prior to", "subsequent to", "in terms of", "with respect to", "in light of"; rewrite "the fact that" out of the sentence; drop "a myriad of", "a plethora of", "paramount", "shed light on", "pave the way for".
